---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import OnDutyCalculator from '../components/OnDutyCalculator.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Where do I set my anchor date for the DuPont schedule?',
    a: 'Enter the first night of a four-night run — that is day 1 of the 28-day cycle this calculator uses (4 nights, 3 off, 3 days, 1 off, 3 nights, 3 off, 4 days, 7 off). If you know a different point of your cycle instead, count back to the start of the last four-night block and use that date.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How often do I get the 7-day break?',
    a: 'Once every 28 days. It is the signature feature of the DuPont rotation: after your four-day stretch, you are off for seven consecutive days — effectively a vacation every month without using leave.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is DuPont hard on sleep?',
    a: 'It is one of the tougher rotations for circadian rhythm because you flip between nights and days inside the same month. The single day off between the three-day and three-night blocks is the widely cited pain point. Most veterans protect the first two days of the 7-day break for recovery sleep and plan family time after that.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many hours per week does DuPont average?',
    a: 'Across the 28-day cycle you work fourteen 12-hour shifts = 168 hours, which averages exactly 42 hours per week. One week in each cycle is a 72-hour week, which is where overtime and fatigue policies get tested.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="DuPont Shift Schedule Calculator — 28-Day Cycle, 12-Month View"
  description="Free DuPont schedule calculator. Enter the first night of a 4-night run and see your full 28-day rotation across 12 months — including the monthly 7-day break, holidays answered, and paydays marked."
  h1="DuPont Schedule Calculator"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Born in DuPont's chemical plants in the 1950s and still standard across refineries, paper mills,
    and power stations, the <strong>DuPont schedule</strong> packs day shifts, night shifts, and one
    glorious seven-day break into every 28-day cycle. It is also the rotation most likely to make you
    lose track of where you are — which is exactly what this calculator fixes.
  </p>

  <OnDutyCalculator defaultPattern="dupont" />

  <h2>The 28-day cycle, decoded</h2>
  <p>
    One full cycle runs: <strong>4 night shifts → 3 off → 3 day shifts → 1 off → 3 night shifts → 3
    off → 4 day shifts → 7 off</strong>. Four crews run the plant, each starting the cycle a week
    apart, so every crew is always at a different chapter of the same story. Fourteen 12-hour shifts
    per cycle works out to a 42-hour weekly average — but lived experience is nothing like an
    average: one week in four you will work 72 hours, and one week you will work zero.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example: anchor your cycle on <em>Monday, September 7</em>. You work nights September
    7–10, rest September 11–13, work days September 14–16, get the single off day on the 17th, work
    nights September 18–20, rest September 21–23, work days September 24–27 — and then October opens
    with your seven-day break, September 28 to October 4. That monthly week off is why crews defend
    DuPont fiercely despite its fatigue profile.
  </p>

  <h2>Living with the hard parts</h2>
  <p>
    The rotation's two pressure points are the <em>one-day turnaround</em> between day and night
    blocks and the <em>72-hour week</em>. Because the calculator lays your whole year out, you can
    see every 72-hour week coming and keep those weeks clear of family commitments, and you can spot
    which direction a shift trade would need to go to protect a specific date. The seven-day breaks
    are also visible months ahead — DuPont workers book flights on break weeks instead of burning
    PTO, and with the 12-month view you can tell exactly which break lines up with a school vacation
    or a hunting season.
  </p>

  <h2>Holidays on DuPont</h2>
  <p>
    With a 28-day cycle drifting through a 365-day year, holiday luck rotates around the four crews
    in a way nobody can eyeball. The holiday list above settles it for the next 12 months. One
    DuPont-specific tip: check whether a "working" holiday falls on your day block or night block —
    a night shift that starts the evening of a holiday often means you still had the daytime
    gathering, which changes whether a swap is worth the favor you will owe.
  </p>
</ToolPage>
